---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle, USE_CASES } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link text-sm font-bold" href="/">← Recovery lab home</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify recovery and retention use cases</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">Choose a playbook by the customer’s situation and the decision your team needs to make. Cart reminders, product education and loyalty benefits have different entry conditions; connecting them requires explicit priority and exit rules.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
      <h2>Find the right starting point</h2>
      <p>If checkout has started, begin with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned cart</a>. If the known shopper only viewed a product, use <a class="link" href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">browse abandonment</a> to address research questions. Neither flow should keep asking for an item after the customer buys it. If the requested variant is unavailable, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">back-in-stock</a> addresses availability rather than pretending checkout is still possible.</p>
      <p>After an order, use post-purchase education for delivery and product-use questions. Replenishment concerns a plausible need for the same consumable; winback concerns a lapse in the relationship. Welcome series explain the store to a new subscriber. These are different reasons to contact someone, not interchangeable delays after a last click.</p>
      <h2>Set the shared operating contract</h2>
      <p>For each flow, record its qualifying event, required identity, channel eligibility, waiting rule, exclusions and exit condition. Name the source of truth for purchases, stock and benefits. Decide what happens when events arrive late or a customer qualifies for multiple flows. Give one owner authority to pause a campaign when the source data is unreliable.</p>
      <p>Choose timing from the product and customer question, then test it as a hypothesis. A high basket value alone does not determine the right delay or discount. Use <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation</a> for reproducible membership and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a> for contribution and offer rules.</p>
      <h2>Handle catalog and seasonal exceptions</h2>
      <p>Size-based products need variant availability; mixed digital and physical orders need distinct access and delivery instructions. Subscription customers may already have a shipment on the way. Gift orders need purchaser and recipient privacy, while wholesale customers may require a sales contact. Consult the <a class="link" href="/for">store-type guides</a> when those constraints shape the workflow.</p>
      <p>For a launch, define whether a waitlist means interest or reserved inventory. For Black Friday, assign an owner to offer start and end states. For holiday gifts, use actual destination cutoffs and fulfillment options. A countdown should never create an availability or delivery promise the store cannot honor.</p>
      <h2>Choose integrations by the job they must do</h2>
      <p>Capture, lifecycle messaging, delivery updates, loyalty accounting and storefront access may have separate owners. Ask each supplier to demonstrate the handoff needed for your selected use case, including a failed update and an export. Do not infer those capabilities from a category label or assume a messaging platform owns the reward ledger. The linked playbooks explain operational requirements; they do not establish a universal app ranking.</p>
      <h2>Validate the workflow and its economics separately</h2>
      <p>Before enabling a flow, propose staff-account exercises for purchase, cancellation, unavailable stock and conflicting benefits. Record expected and observed behavior; no exercise described here is a completed merchant test. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides">operator guides</a> for related implementation questions.</p>
      <p>For causal measurement, define an eligible population, assignment rule and observation window before comparing messages with a holdout. Include customers who do not buy. Reconcile discounts and refunds once in net revenue, then deduct relevant product, fulfillment, reward and channel costs for contribution. Choose sample size and duration for the question; there is no universal holdout percentage. <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">Analytics</a> addresses measurement, while deliverability addresses whether messages can reach their intended audience. Attributed revenue alone settles neither question.</p>
    </article>
    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {USE_CASES.map((u) => (
        <a class="card recovery-card" href={`/use-cases/${u.slug}`}>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-black">{u.name}</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">{u.job}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
